1031
00:42:29,067 --> 00:42:30,733
I, uh...

1032
00:42:30,767 --> 00:42:33,233
I puked cocoa puffs
and hot chocolate

1033
00:42:33,267 --> 00:42:35,033
into Kevin McConnell's
jacket--

1034
00:42:35,067 --> 00:42:36,667
no.

1035
00:42:36,700 --> 00:42:38,133
Uh, is it poop-related?

1036
00:42:38,167 --> 00:42:39,833
Clayton, you know
very well what it is,

1037
00:42:39,867 --> 00:42:41,300
and I think
you will feel better

1038
00:42:41,333 --> 00:42:43,800
if you just admit it
and apologize.

1039
00:42:43,833 --> 00:42:45,700
Mother, make it stop.

1040
00:42:45,733 --> 00:42:47,900
Clayton Deborah

1041
00:42:47,933 --> 00:42:50,433
Susan forrester.

1042
00:42:50,467 --> 00:42:52,567
All right, mom--
all right, come on.

1043
00:42:52,600 --> 00:42:54,500
You and me, come on.
We will dance. Come on.

1044
00:42:54,533 --> 00:42:56,933
You want a piece of me?
Huh? Come on.

1045
00:42:56,967 --> 00:42:59,700
Huh?

1046
00:42:59,733 --> 00:43:01,500
Hah!

1047
00:43:03,300 --> 00:43:05,133
- [Gunshot]
- Ow! Jeez!

1048
00:43:05,167 --> 00:43:08,600
Ahh. Oh.

1049
00:43:08,633 --> 00:43:11,233
Oh! Jeez!

1050
00:43:11,267 --> 00:43:14,300
- [Gunshot]
- D'oh! Nice shooting,
mother.

1051
00:43:14,333 --> 00:43:16,000
- [Gunshot]
- Oh!

1052
00:43:16,033 --> 00:43:19,167
Mother... blood.

1053
00:43:19,200 --> 00:43:20,633
Aren't you hooligans

1054
00:43:20,667 --> 00:43:22,800
due back into the theater?

1055
00:43:22,833 --> 00:43:24,767
- [All] Aah!
- [Alarm blares]

1056
00:43:24,800 --> 00:43:27,433
[All shout]

1057
00:43:39,533 --> 00:43:41,867
Seven hours, and his blood
is still alive.

1058
00:43:44,633 --> 00:43:47,800
Steve, you can still lead
a full and productive life.

1059
00:43:47,833 --> 00:43:51,700
Just reminded of when
my turtle died, that's all.

1060
00:43:51,733 --> 00:43:52,933
[Mike sighs]

1061
00:43:52,967 --> 00:43:54,900
What I'm about to say
might sound strange,

1062
00:43:54,933 --> 00:43:56,800
but I think we should
eat this corpse.

1063
00:43:56,833 --> 00:43:58,433
[Servo chuckles]

1064
00:43:58,467 --> 00:44:00,000
One of US will have to drive
to the nearest telephone

1065
00:44:00,033 --> 00:44:02,733
and call the cape.
Every minute counts.

1066
00:44:02,767 --> 00:44:05,267
- What is it?
- There's a cartoon
in the microscope.

1067
00:44:05,300 --> 00:44:07,800
I've seen amoebic dominance
of the cell structure before.

1068
00:44:07,833 --> 00:44:10,433
But this is completely
out of proportion.

1069
00:44:10,467 --> 00:44:12,300
Well, what are they?
What do they mean?

1070
00:44:12,333 --> 00:44:15,200
Well, normal blood
has two basic cells.

1071
00:44:15,233 --> 00:44:17,600
Duh.

1072
00:44:17,633 --> 00:44:19,567
And the white
fight infection.

1073
00:44:19,600 --> 00:44:21,500
- But this blood has three.
- Hmm.

1074
00:44:21,533 --> 00:44:23,100
The third cell,
that big one.

1075
00:44:23,133 --> 00:44:25,033
Remember that one?
The big one? He had the hair?

1076
00:44:25,067 --> 00:44:27,733
That's completely foreign
to any blood structure.

1077
00:44:27,767 --> 00:44:29,900
God, I'm in a funk.

1078
00:44:29,933 --> 00:44:31,933
If that bacterioid
is contagious,

1079
00:44:31,967 --> 00:44:33,433
then we've all been exposed.

1080
00:44:33,467 --> 00:44:35,467
- Bacterioid?
- I doubt if it's communicable.

1081
00:44:35,500 --> 00:44:37,400
We'd better get
to the nearest town fast.

1082
00:44:37,433 --> 00:44:39,433
- Okay, bye.
- I imagine that'd be
desert city.

1083
00:44:39,467 --> 00:44:40,900
But that's over 30 miles.

1084
00:44:40,933 --> 00:44:42,067
Isn't there anything closer?

1085
00:44:42,100 --> 00:44:43,600
There's gotta be
a pizza hut nearby.

1086
00:44:43,633 --> 00:44:46,067
There's nothing on the map.

1087
00:44:46,100 --> 00:44:47,833
- Steve--
- I'm gonna kill myself.

1088
00:44:47,867 --> 00:44:50,000
Call the cape
from desert city.

1089
00:44:50,033 --> 00:44:51,800
Get in touch with, uh,

1090
00:44:51,833 --> 00:44:53,100
Dr. Zimmerman.

1091
00:44:53,133 --> 00:44:55,100
Don't go into detail.

1092
00:44:55,133 --> 00:44:57,000
Just tell them to get here
as quickly as they can.

1093
00:44:57,033 --> 00:44:58,133
And what about equipment?

1094
00:44:58,167 --> 00:44:59,567
Yeah, get some of that too,
I suppose.

1095
00:44:59,600 --> 00:45:01,633
Well, we need
a new power supply.

1096
00:45:01,667 --> 00:45:03,600
A refrigerated truck
or an ambulance

1097
00:45:03,633 --> 00:45:05,500
to transport the body.

1098
00:45:05,533 --> 00:45:07,433
- Oh, and uh--
- get yourself something nice.

1099
00:45:07,467 --> 00:45:09,133
Tell him to request
priority clearance

1100
00:45:09,167 --> 00:45:10,733
directly to Walter Reed
hospital in Washington.

1101
00:45:10,767 --> 00:45:13,000
Right. I'll be back
as soon as I can.

1102
00:45:13,033 --> 00:45:14,433
Oh, and, uh,
snacks and brews,

1103
00:45:14,467 --> 00:45:16,800
a couple of sandwiches,
and a thing of waxworms.

1104
00:45:16,833 --> 00:45:19,133
Keep checking
the blood pressure, doctor.

1105
00:45:19,167 --> 00:45:22,133
The last count
was 140 over 80.

1106
00:45:22,167 --> 00:45:24,300
What a selfish thing to say.

1107
00:45:24,333 --> 00:45:25,567
That's all we can do
at the present.

1108
00:45:25,600 --> 00:45:27,700
I'm gonna go walk
in front of a bus.

1109
00:45:27,733 --> 00:45:29,900
What's the point
of all this?

1110
00:45:29,933 --> 00:45:32,000
Look at those
little guys go.

1111
00:45:35,333 --> 00:45:38,833
Would you turn
the light off in the hall?

1112
00:45:38,867 --> 00:45:40,233
Oh, so, like,
a blood beast

1113
00:45:40,267 --> 00:45:43,533
can dismantle
a distributor cap?

1114
00:45:43,567 --> 00:45:46,567
Time to dance, blood beast.

1115
00:45:46,600 --> 00:45:49,100
I bet it's those nuns
from the sound of music.

1116
00:45:51,400 --> 00:45:54,533
Steve?

1117
00:45:54,567 --> 00:45:58,100
Steve?

1118
00:45:58,133 --> 00:46:00,233
Any luck with the Jeep?

1119
00:46:00,267 --> 00:46:02,567
I can't figure it out.

1120
00:46:02,600 --> 00:46:05,500
Coils in both the truck
and the Jeep are burnt out.

1121
00:46:05,533 --> 00:46:07,367
That's the most insulting thing
you've ever said to me.

1122
00:46:09,967 --> 00:46:11,267
Hmm.

1123
00:46:11,300 --> 00:46:14,067
I suppose
I should apologize.

1124
00:46:14,100 --> 00:46:15,533
Yeow!

1125
00:46:15,567 --> 00:46:16,733
Oh, oh.

1126
00:46:16,767 --> 00:46:18,567
Anyway,
thanks for listening.

1127
00:46:21,000 --> 00:46:23,967
Look, we missed
the first half of friends.

1128
00:46:24,000 --> 00:46:25,400
They're both stopped.

1129
00:46:25,433 --> 00:46:28,700
Everything's going wrong
in this movie.

1130
00:46:28,733 --> 00:46:32,333
He's an odd little man.

1131
00:46:32,367 --> 00:46:35,733
I just developed a picture,
everyone.

1132
00:46:35,767 --> 00:46:37,467
Um, if you're looking
for that one thing,

1133
00:46:37,500 --> 00:46:40,467
Barb moved it.

1134
00:46:40,500 --> 00:46:42,633
Steve, Steve and I have
something to tell you.

1135
00:46:45,133 --> 00:46:47,100
- Take a look.
- This was in my tuna.

1136
00:46:47,133 --> 00:46:48,533
We're in a magnetic
force field.

1137
00:46:48,567 --> 00:46:51,200
- A what?
- A net of static electricity

1138
00:46:51,233 --> 00:46:52,700
is surrounding
the entire area.

1139
00:46:52,733 --> 00:46:54,600
- That's why
our power's dead.
- Mm-hmm.

1140
00:46:54,633 --> 00:46:56,733
Well, we're not going
anywhere tonight.

1141
00:46:56,767 --> 00:46:59,533
The truck and the Jeep
are burned out electrically.

1142
00:46:59,567 --> 00:47:01,533
What would cause
a thing like that?

1143
00:47:01,567 --> 00:47:03,900
- God hates US.
- There's a very powerful
magnetic source

1144
00:47:03,933 --> 00:47:05,533
- somewhere nearby.
- Oh.

1145
00:47:05,567 --> 00:47:08,200
It's reversing all positive
electrical power.

1146
00:47:08,233 --> 00:47:10,200
You sly boots.

1147
00:47:10,233 --> 00:47:13,067
Dr. Wyman, how close
is the satellite compartment?

1148
00:47:13,100 --> 00:47:15,167
- Who cares?
- Two, maybe three miles.

1149
00:47:15,200 --> 00:47:17,633
Just thinking, is it possible
that the compartment

1150
00:47:17,667 --> 00:47:20,033
could be bombarded
with electron particles?

1151
00:47:20,067 --> 00:47:21,767
It could be.
It's happened before.

1152
00:47:21,800 --> 00:47:24,533
- On some of the earlier
smart farms--
- snarf farm?

1153
00:47:24,567 --> 00:47:26,000
When the missiles
came back to earth,

1154
00:47:26,033 --> 00:47:27,800
- they were pretty
heavily magnetized.
- [All giggle]

1155
00:47:27,833 --> 00:47:30,100
There's a mountain range
between US and the compartment.

1156
00:47:30,133 --> 00:47:31,600
Yeah, well, there
doesn't seem to be

1157
00:47:31,633 --> 00:47:33,133
any logic
behind any of this.

1158
00:47:33,167 --> 00:47:35,700
Well, here's something to
complicate things even more.

1159
00:47:35,733 --> 00:47:37,100
I took these upside-down.

1160
00:47:37,133 --> 00:47:39,700
Look at the shot I took when
Dave and I first got out there.

1161
00:47:39,733 --> 00:47:43,133
Look at it closely.

1162
00:47:43,167 --> 00:47:45,300
Dave, remember I mentioned
the strange mud?

1163
00:47:45,333 --> 00:47:46,667
- Yes?
- Hmm?

1164
00:47:46,700 --> 00:47:49,000
Well, this was
the last shot taken.

1165
00:47:49,033 --> 00:47:50,400
Most of the mud
has disappeared,

1166
00:47:50,433 --> 00:47:52,133
- and the opening is larger.
- Hmm.

1167
00:47:52,167 --> 00:47:53,833
Yes, I remember that
when we took John

1168
00:47:53,867 --> 00:47:55,367
out of the compartment.

1169
00:47:55,400 --> 00:47:58,400
But at the time,
it didn't seem important.

1170
00:47:58,433 --> 00:48:00,167
Someone or something
must've been there.

1171
00:48:00,200 --> 00:48:02,300
Saw a huge,
blood-sucking alien,

1172
00:48:02,333 --> 00:48:04,800
but I didn't think
it was important, either.

1173
00:48:04,833 --> 00:48:07,533
Ah, I'm gonna
go kill myself again.

1174
00:48:07,567 --> 00:48:09,300
I don't know why...

1175
00:48:09,333 --> 00:48:12,300
Steve? Steve?

1176
00:48:12,333 --> 00:48:14,133
Hey, anyone
remember pringles?

1177
00:48:14,167 --> 00:48:16,200
There's an explanation
and a good one for all of this.

1178
00:48:16,233 --> 00:48:18,467
- Elves.
- There must be.

1179
00:48:18,500 --> 00:48:19,967
Hold on, i--
I guess there's not.

1180
00:48:20,000 --> 00:48:21,400
Some of the things
that have occurred today

1181
00:48:21,433 --> 00:48:22,767
or without precedent.

1182
00:48:22,800 --> 00:48:24,333
Ah, just ignore me.

1183
00:48:24,367 --> 00:48:26,733
There's a man in there alive
that should be dead.

1184
00:48:26,767 --> 00:48:28,733
Something that's
never happened before.

1185
00:48:28,767 --> 00:48:30,867
Well, once, but he had
the wind at his back.

1186
00:48:30,900 --> 00:48:33,100
We've never sent a man
so far into space before--

1187
00:48:33,133 --> 00:48:35,933
with so little food
and no air.

1188
00:48:35,967 --> 00:48:38,533
Well, we're all tired.

1189
00:48:38,567 --> 00:48:40,567
- Disappointed--
- stupid.

1190
00:48:40,600 --> 00:48:42,100
We're under
a terrible strain.

1191
00:48:42,133 --> 00:48:44,133
I feel good.

1192
00:48:44,167 --> 00:48:45,967
After this long,
the cape has probably

1193
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:48,267
contacted the Pentagon.
It's just a matter of time.

1194
00:48:48,300 --> 00:48:49,767
What if they
can't get through?

1195
00:48:49,800 --> 00:48:51,767
The magnetic force-field
works both ways.

1196
00:48:51,800 --> 00:48:53,100
Okay, so I'm wrong.

1197
00:48:53,133 --> 00:48:54,533
Well, in the morning, one of US
will have to hike down

1198
00:48:54,567 --> 00:48:56,000
and meet them.

1199
00:48:56,033 --> 00:48:58,067
As a precautionary measure,
I suggest we all

1200
00:48:58,100 --> 00:49:00,367
- stay in this room tonight.
- I'll lay on Sharon.

1201
00:49:00,400 --> 00:49:03,733
Dave and I will take turns
watching corcoran.

1202
00:49:03,767 --> 00:49:06,200
Hey, you're taller
than last year, honey.

1203
00:49:06,233 --> 00:49:07,567
[Julie sobs]

1204
00:49:07,600 --> 00:49:11,333
- Condescend, condescend.
- Julie. Julie.

1205
00:49:11,367 --> 00:49:14,333
I'm sorry, doctor.

1206
00:49:14,367 --> 00:49:15,933
It's just, I can't
hold it back any longer.

1207
00:49:15,967 --> 00:49:18,767
I have to overact.

1208
00:49:18,800 --> 00:49:20,133
I wish there was
something I could say.

1209
00:49:20,167 --> 00:49:22,000
I guess I just said that.

1210
00:49:22,033 --> 00:49:24,333
I'll listen if you like.

1211
00:49:24,367 --> 00:49:26,667
* girl, you'll be
a woman soon *

1212
00:49:26,700 --> 00:49:28,967
--losing someone you love.

1213
00:49:30,833 --> 00:49:33,467
The unknown, and being--

1214
00:49:33,500 --> 00:49:35,433
[servo sobs wildly]

1215
00:49:35,467 --> 00:49:37,633
Alex, I don't--

1216
00:49:37,667 --> 00:49:39,533
you know, my gown opens
from the rear.

1217
00:49:39,567 --> 00:49:42,400
Julie.

1218
00:49:42,433 --> 00:49:44,133
Say, hello.

1219
00:49:44,167 --> 00:49:46,067
Julie?

1220
00:49:46,100 --> 00:49:48,533
He alone stood on
the threshold of our future.

1221
00:49:48,567 --> 00:49:50,733
And he peed it down his leg.

1222
00:49:50,767 --> 00:49:52,067
What happened to him today,

1223
00:49:52,100 --> 00:49:54,600
we may learn more
than we've ever known.

1224
00:49:54,633 --> 00:49:57,300
We've learned not to send
polacks into space.

1225
00:49:57,333 --> 00:49:59,033
Whoo.

1226
00:49:59,067 --> 00:50:02,100
Maybe I should just listen.

1227
00:50:02,133 --> 00:50:03,567
There'll be other steves.

1228
00:50:03,600 --> 00:50:05,033
He's already gone...

1229
00:50:05,067 --> 00:50:07,867
Ooh, mama, that is so sweet.

1230
00:50:09,900 --> 00:50:12,500
But is he dead?

1231
00:50:12,533 --> 00:50:15,433
Look, let's not make
a federal case out of this.

1232
00:50:16,900 --> 00:50:19,367
Well, the listening thing
went over like a lead balloon.

1233
00:50:21,867 --> 00:50:23,633
Scientists--
the more we learn,

1234
00:50:23,667 --> 00:50:25,100
the more we realize
there is to learn.

1235
00:50:25,133 --> 00:50:27,833
- Scientists?
- Life itself is
the greatest miracle.

1236
00:50:27,867 --> 00:50:29,767
Well...

1237
00:50:29,800 --> 00:50:31,400
When we get him
to the hospital tomorrow,

1238
00:50:31,433 --> 00:50:33,167
maybe we'll know.

1239
00:50:33,200 --> 00:50:35,500
Uh, oh, my adenoids, ugh.

1240
00:50:39,533 --> 00:50:41,800
Could you massage
my front now?

1241
00:50:41,833 --> 00:50:44,433
- Good night, doctor.
- Good night, Julie.

1242
00:50:44,467 --> 00:50:47,000
Just three little ties
in the back, slips right off.

1243
00:50:47,033 --> 00:50:48,967
[Mike laughs]

1244
00:50:49,000 --> 00:50:51,033
Well, I just needed
to talk to somebody.

1245
00:50:51,067 --> 00:50:52,967
I've been a little down.
Thanks a lot.

1246
00:50:54,933 --> 00:50:57,167
You need one of these, huh?

1247
00:51:03,733 --> 00:51:07,200
Oh, here comes
the nutty part.

1248
00:51:07,233 --> 00:51:09,333
[Servo mimics
whoopie cushion]

1249
00:51:09,367 --> 00:51:11,967
- That's an easy one.
- Oh, come on.

1250
00:51:12,000 --> 00:51:13,433
Let's see where--
"twas brillig

1251
00:51:13,467 --> 00:51:14,833
and the slithy toves,"

1252
00:51:14,867 --> 00:51:17,167
oh, what the hell
am I thinking?

1253
00:51:18,633 --> 00:51:19,933
Martha Graham!

1254
00:51:19,967 --> 00:51:22,033
Who put my sweater
in the dryer?

1255
00:51:26,867 --> 00:51:29,733
[Low creaking]

1256
00:51:29,767 --> 00:51:31,433
Sorry.

1257
00:51:31,467 --> 00:51:33,133
Wait...

1258
00:51:33,167 --> 00:51:35,167
There's one last piece
of carrot cake,

1259
00:51:35,200 --> 00:51:36,633
and I'm gonna have it.

1260
00:51:36,667 --> 00:51:38,200
[Servo chuckles]

1261
00:51:40,733 --> 00:51:42,467
Hey.

1262
00:51:42,500 --> 00:51:43,800
Did you put
the blood beast out?

1263
00:51:43,833 --> 00:51:45,700
There's something in there.

1264
00:51:48,033 --> 00:51:49,333
Be vewy quiet.

1265
00:51:49,367 --> 00:51:50,700
We're hunting bwood beasts.

1266
00:51:50,733 --> 00:51:52,067
[Elmer Fudd chuckle]

1267
00:51:52,100 --> 00:51:53,600
The lights are out.

1268
00:51:53,633 --> 00:51:56,967
Loin chop?
No, I don't think so.

1269
00:51:57,000 --> 00:51:58,800
Huh?

1270
00:51:58,833 --> 00:52:02,400
Ew, the patient
has blown a gasket.

1271
00:52:02,433 --> 00:52:04,033
Yuck.

1272
00:52:04,067 --> 00:52:07,533
Hi, we're looking for
an honest man.

1273
00:52:07,567 --> 00:52:10,300
- Dr. Wyman.
- Put some pants on.

1274
00:52:10,333 --> 00:52:11,700
--His head's gone.

1275
00:52:11,733 --> 00:52:14,800
No, that's
his hinder, Steve.

1276
00:52:14,833 --> 00:52:17,233
Yep, it's the dismount
that always impales him.

1277
00:52:17,267 --> 00:52:19,033
Wow.

1278
00:52:19,067 --> 00:52:20,467
Dr. Wyman!

1279
00:52:20,500 --> 00:52:23,867
You march straight down
from there, mister.

1280
00:52:23,900 --> 00:52:25,067
There's nothing you can do.

1281
00:52:25,100 --> 00:52:29,067
Unless you know
where his face is.

1282
00:52:29,100 --> 00:52:31,800
Another long scene
in here should help.

1283
00:52:31,833 --> 00:52:34,200
Must've taken the body.

1284
00:52:34,233 --> 00:52:35,667
Whatever it is,
it works fast.

1285
00:52:35,700 --> 00:52:38,200
- Provides soothing relief.
- Let's take him down.

1286
00:52:38,233 --> 00:52:39,967
You said he could
stay up till new year's,

1287
00:52:40,000 --> 00:52:42,067
and that means
Christmas is over.

1288
00:52:44,333 --> 00:52:45,733
I forgot to tell him
that Rhonda called.

1289
00:52:45,767 --> 00:52:48,867
That Rhonda called.

1290
00:52:48,900 --> 00:52:50,967
Oh god, I slipped
on his face, ew.

1291
00:52:51,000 --> 00:52:52,933
Oh. Oh god.

1292
00:52:54,300 --> 00:52:57,800
Yeah, I get that a lot.

1293
00:52:57,833 --> 00:53:00,533
Oh, I just blew all over
the operating room.

1294
00:53:00,567 --> 00:53:02,233
[Servo retches]

1295
00:53:05,300 --> 00:53:06,667
Well, welcome back

1296
00:53:06,700 --> 00:53:08,667
to the land
of the living, mister.

1297
00:53:11,433 --> 00:53:13,567
- Steve.
- What, he-Steve
or me-Steve?

1298
00:53:13,600 --> 00:53:14,933
Help him to the table.

1299
00:53:14,967 --> 00:53:16,567
Let's shoot him
back into space

1300
00:53:16,600 --> 00:53:18,700
and see what happens.

1301
00:53:18,733 --> 00:53:21,500
How about hello?
Hi? Anything?

1302
00:53:22,967 --> 00:53:24,267
Johnny.

1303
00:53:24,300 --> 00:53:26,167
Oh, Steve's name is Johnny.

1304
00:53:26,200 --> 00:53:27,700
You're all right.

1305
00:53:27,733 --> 00:53:30,067
Julie...

1306
00:53:30,100 --> 00:53:31,833
I don't know.

1307
00:53:31,867 --> 00:53:33,300
I don't know.

1308
00:53:33,333 --> 00:53:34,500
I think so.

1309
00:53:34,533 --> 00:53:37,733
Can I have an eskimo pie?

1310
00:53:37,767 --> 00:53:39,167
Where's Dr. Wyman?

1311
00:53:39,200 --> 00:53:40,467
I hadn't finished his head.

1312
00:53:43,167 --> 00:53:44,633
What happened?

1313
00:53:44,667 --> 00:53:47,967
Suppose you tell US
what happened.

1314
00:53:48,000 --> 00:53:50,100
Julie, what happened
to Dr. Wyman?

1315
00:53:50,133 --> 00:53:51,300
He's dead.

1316
00:53:51,333 --> 00:53:53,633
Thank you, Julie.

1317
00:53:53,667 --> 00:53:56,133
- Hey, wait up.
- Why are you staring at me?

1318
00:53:58,633 --> 00:54:00,367
You don't think that I had
anything to do with it.

1319
00:54:00,400 --> 00:54:01,867
- Johnny.
- Aka Steve.

1320
00:54:01,900 --> 00:54:03,767
We're just as confused
as you are.

1321
00:54:03,800 --> 00:54:05,200
Did you see anything?

1322
00:54:05,233 --> 00:54:08,433
Was there anybody in the room
with you and Dr. Wyman?

1323
00:54:08,467 --> 00:54:09,833
I don't know.

1324
00:54:09,867 --> 00:54:13,167
I don't wanna hear
"I don't know," mister!

1325
00:54:13,200 --> 00:54:14,467
Last thing I remember--

1326
00:54:14,500 --> 00:54:15,967
* I was headed for the door

1327
00:54:16,000 --> 00:54:17,400
I was making my descent.

1328
00:54:17,433 --> 00:54:18,900
The jets didn't take hold--
when I finally

1329
00:54:18,933 --> 00:54:22,367
released the drag chute,
it didn't hold.

1330
00:54:22,400 --> 00:54:23,600
I was too close to the ground.

1331
00:54:23,633 --> 00:54:27,200
You crashed.

1332
00:54:27,233 --> 00:54:28,467
You're so full of it.

1333
00:54:28,500 --> 00:54:30,300
You don't remember?

1334
00:54:30,333 --> 00:54:33,067
I seemed to be floating.

1335
00:54:33,100 --> 00:54:36,133
In a warm, black mist.

1336
00:54:36,167 --> 00:54:38,900
Ah, we gave you
a pudding bath.

1337
00:54:38,933 --> 00:54:40,267
Oh, my head--

1338
00:54:40,300 --> 00:54:41,767
my achy, breaky head.

1339
00:54:41,800 --> 00:54:43,300
- My neck.
- Have you seen it?

1340
00:54:43,333 --> 00:54:44,800
Oh, no.

1341
00:54:44,833 --> 00:54:46,800
Look at that.

1342
00:54:46,833 --> 00:54:49,867
- The same Mark.
- I told him not
to pick at that.

1343
00:54:51,033 --> 00:54:54,333
Oh, did that thing pop?

1344
00:54:54,367 --> 00:54:55,933
Hey, it's old pizza back.

1345
00:54:55,967 --> 00:54:57,433
[All laugh]

1346
00:54:57,467 --> 00:55:01,200
See, I got a little nutty
with a wood chisel.

1347
00:55:01,233 --> 00:55:02,633
What's this?

1348
00:55:02,667 --> 00:55:04,200
You think I'm Steve,
don't you?

1349
00:55:06,633 --> 00:55:07,867
Tell me what's happening.

1350
00:55:07,900 --> 00:55:10,200
John...

1351
00:55:10,233 --> 00:55:12,467
You don't understand.

1352
00:55:12,500 --> 00:55:13,800
We thought you were dead.

1353
00:55:13,833 --> 00:55:17,700
And, frankly, we're getting
pretty used to the idea.

1354
00:55:17,733 --> 00:55:19,033
Yeah, we sold off
your organs.

1355
00:55:19,067 --> 00:55:20,333
What do you mean, dead?

1356
00:55:20,367 --> 00:55:22,033
Don't yell at US.

1357
00:55:22,067 --> 00:55:23,767
When we pulled you
out of the compartment,

1358
00:55:23,800 --> 00:55:26,167
your body had completely
ceased to function.

1359
00:55:26,200 --> 00:55:28,067
What do you mean,
compartment?

1360
00:55:31,600 --> 00:55:33,400
A hypometabolistic state.

1361
00:55:33,433 --> 00:55:35,167
- Oh, no.
- A type of...

1362
00:55:35,200 --> 00:55:37,133
- Suspended animation.
- No, no-no-no.

1363
00:55:37,167 --> 00:55:38,467
- You were dead.
- Brought on...

1364
00:55:38,500 --> 00:55:40,433
By the contraction
of the mesentary blood vessels.

1365
00:55:40,467 --> 00:55:42,800
It's so sad.

1366
00:55:42,833 --> 00:55:45,567
That kind of terminology--
I'd expect it from Dr. Wyman,

1367
00:55:45,600 --> 00:55:47,833
- not from you.
- You wash your mouth out
with soap.

1368
00:55:47,867 --> 00:55:49,933
Well, it seemed like
the natural diagnosis.

1369
00:55:49,967 --> 00:55:52,500
From Dr. Wyman, yes.

1370
00:55:52,533 --> 00:55:54,733
In some way,
I think Dr. Wyman

1371
00:55:54,767 --> 00:55:56,667
is influencing me.

1372
00:55:56,700 --> 00:55:58,033
I think he may
be a part of me now.

1373
00:55:58,067 --> 00:55:59,400
Can you be dead again?

1374
00:55:59,433 --> 00:56:02,133
[Servo chuckles]

1375
00:56:02,167 --> 00:56:03,467
I don't really think that.

1376
00:56:03,500 --> 00:56:06,567
I don't understand.

1377
00:56:06,600 --> 00:56:08,567
You've got to help me!

1378
00:56:08,600 --> 00:56:09,967
Help me!

1379
00:56:10,000 --> 00:56:11,300
Fantastic--
it's unbelievable.

1380
00:56:11,333 --> 00:56:13,167
Things like this
just don't happen.

1381
00:56:13,200 --> 00:56:15,000
This is helping.

1382
00:56:15,033 --> 00:56:18,000
[Crow groans]

1383
00:56:18,033 --> 00:56:19,267
Thank you.

1384
00:56:19,300 --> 00:56:20,600
Really.

1385
00:56:20,633 --> 00:56:23,767
Thank you.

1386
00:56:23,800 --> 00:56:25,300
I'm not afraid.

1387
00:56:25,333 --> 00:56:27,867
- I'm not afraid.
- Afraid not am I.

1388
00:56:27,900 --> 00:56:30,067
It didn't come here
to destroy.

1389
00:56:30,100 --> 00:56:32,933
Johnny, try to tell US.

1390
00:56:32,967 --> 00:56:35,300
What didn't come here
to destroy US?

1391
00:56:35,333 --> 00:56:36,867
No more questions.
More boobies.

1392
00:56:36,900 --> 00:56:38,167
[Crow sobs]

1393
00:56:38,200 --> 00:56:39,933
He all right?

1394
00:56:43,667 --> 00:56:45,767
Oh, this one's not as nice.

1395
00:56:48,567 --> 00:56:51,033
- They're gone.
- My brownies are gone.

1396
00:56:52,867 --> 00:56:55,633
I've got a bassoon
if we need one.

1397
00:56:59,100 --> 00:57:01,267
What are you talking about?

1398
00:57:01,300 --> 00:57:02,533
Come see for yourself.

1399
00:57:02,567 --> 00:57:04,200
You big, dumb, dead guy.

1400
00:57:04,233 --> 00:57:06,633
Sit down.

1401
00:57:06,667 --> 00:57:08,967
[All mimic
car engines, horns]

1402
00:57:09,000 --> 00:57:11,333
Normal in every respect.

1403
00:57:11,367 --> 00:57:13,467
Four hours ago, it was populated

1404
00:57:13,500 --> 00:57:15,400
with alien amorphic
cell structures.

1405
00:57:15,433 --> 00:57:17,667
They mean to win wimbledon.

1406
00:57:19,533 --> 00:57:22,100
I think we should see
what I look like under the
fluoroscope.

1407
00:57:22,133 --> 00:57:23,667
Yeah, that'll
kill some time.

1408
00:57:23,700 --> 00:57:25,267
[Crow giggles]

1409
00:57:25,300 --> 00:57:27,800
Put a shirt on while
you're at it.

1410
00:57:27,833 --> 00:57:29,933
How will it work
with the power out?

1411
00:57:29,967 --> 00:57:32,033
It operates on
a radium cathode tube.

1412
00:57:32,067 --> 00:57:34,633
Oh.

1413
00:57:34,667 --> 00:57:37,033
Wow, he's loaded
with shrimp.

1414
00:57:37,067 --> 00:57:39,267
[All laugh]

1415
00:57:39,300 --> 00:57:41,733
The amorphic cell structures
have expanded to--

1416
00:57:41,767 --> 00:57:43,033
Jacksonville!

1417
00:57:43,067 --> 00:57:45,233
They're using his body
for a breeding ground.

1418
00:57:45,267 --> 00:57:46,833
You're not gonna
harm them, are you?

1419
00:57:46,867 --> 00:57:48,333
What do you mean?

1420
00:57:48,367 --> 00:57:51,067
We don't even know
what they are.

1421
00:57:51,100 --> 00:57:52,800
The one thing we do know
is that they're alien

1422
00:57:52,833 --> 00:57:54,700
- to the human body.
- Young man.

1423
00:57:54,733 --> 00:57:56,733
Somehow, they must've come down
with you in the compartment.

1424
00:57:56,767 --> 00:57:58,533
You're gross.

1425
00:57:58,567 --> 00:58:00,833
They were microscopic.

1426
00:58:00,867 --> 00:58:02,300
We've got to do something.

1427
00:58:02,333 --> 00:58:04,933
If you destroy them,

1428
00:58:04,967 --> 00:58:06,200
you destroy me.

1429
00:58:06,233 --> 00:58:07,600
* steves don't preach

1430
00:58:07,633 --> 00:58:10,167
* I'm in trouble deep

1431
00:58:10,200 --> 00:58:11,633
you know what?
I think I get

1432
00:58:11,667 --> 00:58:14,567
hush puppies and Cole slaw
with these.

1433
00:58:14,600 --> 00:58:15,833
You can't destroy them.

1434
00:58:15,867 --> 00:58:19,167
- I'll take care of them.
- Why not?

1435
00:58:19,200 --> 00:58:20,900
That's why not.

1436
00:58:20,933 --> 00:58:23,533
Kramer! I love him.

1437
00:58:23,567 --> 00:58:24,867
Barney's been in a fire!

1438
00:58:24,900 --> 00:58:26,167
* I love you

1439
00:58:26,200 --> 00:58:28,167
* you burned me

1440
00:58:28,200 --> 00:58:31,333
Steve, don't shoot,
it won't hurt you!

1441
00:58:31,367 --> 00:58:32,700
- Steve, don't shoot!
- Stop shooting.

1442
00:58:32,733 --> 00:58:34,200
Don't shoot!
It won't hurt you!

1443
00:58:34,233 --> 00:58:37,600
This is no way
to meet the in-laws.

1444
00:58:37,633 --> 00:58:39,967
Ah boy, you know,
you marry the whole family, huh?

1445
00:58:40,000 --> 00:58:43,833
- Yeah, yeah.
- That's true.

1446
00:58:43,867 --> 00:58:46,400
Not the copier!

1447
00:58:46,433 --> 00:58:49,800
My resume was in there!

1448
00:58:49,833 --> 00:58:51,167
Hey, look, I'm really sorry.

1449
00:58:51,200 --> 00:58:53,367
I thought Johnny told you
I was dropping by.

1450
00:58:56,233 --> 00:58:58,767
Oh, oh boy.

1451
00:58:58,800 --> 00:59:02,167
Can't stop me from
seeing my children.

1452
00:59:02,200 --> 00:59:04,267
Come back here, you varmink!

1453
00:59:06,133 --> 00:59:09,267
You stupid wall!
Stupid wall!

1454
00:59:09,300 --> 00:59:11,533
Stupid, stupid wall.

1455
00:59:16,200 --> 00:59:17,500
Come here, you little tramp.

1456
00:59:17,533 --> 00:59:18,800
When I think of
the blood beast's hands

1457
00:59:18,833 --> 00:59:21,133
all over you...

1458
00:59:21,167 --> 00:59:22,867
It's burned, but it got away,
didn't it?

1459
00:59:22,900 --> 00:59:24,267
It didn't go very far.

1460
00:59:24,300 --> 00:59:26,400
It's gone.
It was burnt pretty bad.

1461
00:59:26,433 --> 00:59:28,433
I think we oughta track it down
and make sure it's dead.

1462
00:59:28,467 --> 00:59:29,767
No, let's wait for help.

1463
00:59:29,800 --> 00:59:32,133
We can't possibly combat
that thing by ourselves.

1464
00:59:32,167 --> 00:59:34,233
Look, nobody's gotten to US,
and nobody probably will

1465
00:59:34,267 --> 00:59:35,533
until it's destroyed.

1466
00:59:35,567 --> 00:59:36,867
Why does it have
to be killed?

1467
00:59:36,900 --> 00:59:38,200
Don't you think he's cute?

1468
00:59:38,233 --> 00:59:39,833
Why are we always
so quick to destroy?

1469
00:59:39,867 --> 00:59:41,200
It's-- it's an alien.

1470
00:59:41,233 --> 00:59:43,100
It's the first of its kind
to come to the earth.

1471
00:59:43,133 --> 00:59:44,600
John, you know what
it's done to you.

1472
00:59:44,633 --> 00:59:45,900
Breeding its young
in your body.

1473
00:59:45,933 --> 00:59:47,400
You saw what it did
to Dr. Wyman.

1474
00:59:47,433 --> 00:59:50,033
Isn't that reason enough?
It has to be killed.

1475
00:59:50,067 --> 00:59:52,667
I don't understand,
it didn't come in malice.

1476
00:59:52,700 --> 00:59:54,300
It could've killed
all of you earlier,

1477
00:59:54,333 --> 00:59:55,633
but it was me it was after.

1478
00:59:55,667 --> 00:59:56,967
And I loved
every minute of it!

1479
00:59:57,000 --> 00:59:59,333
It's gotta come back to me.

1480
00:59:59,367 --> 01:00:01,233
- To nourish its young.
- That's weird, Steve.

1481
01:00:01,267 --> 01:00:03,233
All the more reason
to destroy it.

1482
01:00:03,267 --> 01:00:05,400
Suppose it is still alive.
Bullets don't harm it.

1483
01:00:05,433 --> 01:00:07,100
I got it handled, Steve.

1484
01:00:07,133 --> 01:00:09,267
Fire seems to.

1485
01:00:09,300 --> 01:00:11,433
A very pistol.
It has an engulfing flame

1486
01:00:11,467 --> 01:00:13,000
plus a delayed explosion.

1487
01:00:13,033 --> 01:00:14,433
- Fairy pistol?
- It's better than a lantern.

1488
01:00:14,467 --> 01:00:16,467
- Very pistol?
- Flare gun.

1489
01:00:16,500 --> 01:00:18,200
At short range,
it'll chop down anything.

1490
01:00:18,233 --> 01:00:19,900
There's some in the cabinet
in the hall, I'll get them.

1491
01:00:19,933 --> 01:00:21,300
No, Dave, you can't!

1492
01:00:21,333 --> 01:00:22,900
Listen, you can't
kill a living creature

1493
01:00:22,933 --> 01:00:26,133
without giving it a chance
to justify itself.

1494
01:00:26,167 --> 01:00:28,333
- [Johnny groans]
- Oh, sorry. Hormones.

1495
01:00:28,367 --> 01:00:29,600
Let's put him in there.

1496
01:00:29,633 --> 01:00:33,200
Ah, yeah,
grandma's old room.

1497
01:00:33,233 --> 01:00:34,533
Well, I'll try to raise them

1498
01:00:34,567 --> 01:00:38,333
as if they're
my very own shrimp.

1499
01:00:38,367 --> 01:00:40,867
Uh, yeah, just lay him
on the ironing board.

1500
01:00:40,900 --> 01:00:42,800
[All laugh]

1501
01:00:45,167 --> 01:00:47,000
So, Julie, guess he wasn't

1502
01:00:47,033 --> 01:00:50,033
getting enough at home, huh?

1503
01:00:50,067 --> 01:00:51,967
- Julie?
- [Mike laughs]

1504
01:00:52,000 --> 01:00:54,567
Can you give him something
to keep him quiet?

1505
01:00:54,600 --> 01:00:56,567
Sodium amytal.

1506
01:00:56,600 --> 01:00:58,233
- Good.
- Or you could just
turn on cartoons.

1507
01:00:58,267 --> 01:00:59,567
We'll leave you here
to watch him.

1508
01:00:59,600 --> 01:01:00,867
Donna will come with US--

1509
01:01:00,900 --> 01:01:02,667
we're gonna need
photographic coverage.

1510
01:01:02,700 --> 01:01:04,700
This is going
right in my eye.

1511
01:01:04,733 --> 01:01:07,233
Will that really keep him out
till we get back?

1512
01:01:07,267 --> 01:01:09,100
I don't know.

1513
01:01:09,133 --> 01:01:10,733
Under normal circumstances,

1514
01:01:10,767 --> 01:01:12,067
this should
render him unconscious

1515
01:01:12,100 --> 01:01:13,400
for at least twelve hours.

1516
01:01:13,433 --> 01:01:14,733
But this isn't
a normal person.

1517
01:01:14,767 --> 01:01:15,867
This is no ordinary Steve.

1518
01:01:15,900 --> 01:01:17,633
What you and he were
to each other.

1519
01:01:17,667 --> 01:01:19,333
Has no bearing on
what he is now, Julie.

1520
01:01:19,367 --> 01:01:21,200
Actually, it was pretty
lukewarm.

1521
01:01:21,233 --> 01:01:22,900
I'm not worried.

1522
01:01:22,933 --> 01:01:24,833
He's not dangerous.

1523
01:01:24,867 --> 01:01:26,200
Not to me, anyway.

1524
01:01:26,233 --> 01:01:28,233
Except when
the packers lose.

1525
01:01:28,267 --> 01:01:29,833
Well, we can't
take any chances.

1526
01:01:29,867 --> 01:01:33,400
That creature eludes US
and comes back here,

1527
01:01:33,433 --> 01:01:36,400
or if he gives you
a bad time,

1528
01:01:36,433 --> 01:01:39,067
you'd better know
how to use a very pistol.

1529
01:01:39,100 --> 01:01:40,400
Believe me, Julie,

1530
01:01:40,433 --> 01:01:42,400
he's not the same man
you were engaged to.

1531
01:01:42,433 --> 01:01:43,833
Here's your
frankenberry pistol.

1532
01:01:43,867 --> 01:01:46,733
Thanks-- let me have one
for Julie, will you?

1533
